useEffect is a React Hook used to perform side effects in functional components.

A side effect is anything that happens outside the normal rendering process.

Examples:

API calls
Fetching data
Timers (setInterval, setTimeout)
Updating document title
Accessing local storage
Adding event listeners





Syntax
<!-- useEffect(() => {
  // Side Effect Code

  return () => {
    // Cleanup Code (optional)
  };
}, [dependencies]); -->


<!-- Structure
useEffect(
    callback function,
    dependency array
)
useEffect(()=>{},[count]) -->







Suppose you want to change the page title whenever the counter changes.

----------❌ Wrong approach

<!-- function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  document.title = `Count ${count}`;

  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
      Increment
    </button>
  );
} -->

This runs every time the component renders.

---------- ✔️Better approach

<!-- useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Count ${count}`;
}, [count]); -->

React now knows exactly when to run this code.











How useEffect Works
Component Render
       ↓
React Updates DOM
       ↓
useEffect Runs

Unlike rendering code, useEffect runs after the component is rendered on the screen.

